import java.lang.reflect.Constructor;

/**
 * TASK: Build a tiny dependency-injection container.
 *
 * build(type) creates an instance of type without the caller ever writing
 * `new`. The rule is deliberately simple:
 *
 *   1. Ask for type.getDeclaredConstructors(). If there is NOT exactly one,
 *      throw new IllegalArgumentException("cannot build " + type.getName()).
 *   2. Otherwise call setAccessible(true) on it and look at
 *      constructor.getParameterTypes().
 *   3. Build EACH parameter type by calling build recursively.
 *   4. Return (T) constructor.newInstance(builtArguments).
 *
 * Because the "exactly one constructor" rule is applied at every level, a type
 * that needs something unbuildable fails naturally: HandTool takes a String,
 * and java.lang.String declares many constructors, so build(HandTool.class)
 * ends up throwing IllegalArgumentException with the message
 * "cannot build java.lang.String".
 *
 * Example:
 *   TinyInjector.build(MotorUnit.class).describe() -> "motor"
 *   TinyInjector.build(RoadCar.class).describe()   -> "car(motor,wheels)"
 *   TinyInjector.build(CarDepot.class).describe()  -> "depot[car(motor,wheels)]"
 *
 * Each call must produce a FRESH object -- nothing is shared or reused.
 */
